## 5. Important architectural decisions

- **One shared collection:** This avoids per-user collection management and keeps collection configuration and operations centralized. Payload filters and indexes provide tenant scoping. Separate collections per user would offer more physical separation, but create more operational overhead and collection proliferation.
- **Page-aware chunks:** Chunking each page separately preserves citation location. This avoids losing page attribution when extracted text is flattened.
- **Stable IDs and replacement on retry:** Deterministic point IDs make repeated ingestion address the same points. Deleting the document’s previous points before replacement also removes stale trailing chunks if a later run produces fewer chunks.
- **Delayed indexing:** New points are not searchable until ingestion has completed. This keeps partially written vectors out of normal retrieval.
- **Non-destructive collection setup:** The previous recreation path could remove or block access to shared data. Collection setup now validates existing configuration and adds missing indexes without deleting points.

## 6. Security considerations

- The API passes the verified JWT user ID into retrieval; the frontend cannot choose the search owner.
- New vector payloads include `user_id` and `document_id`; API retrieval filters by the authenticated user and `indexed=true`.
- Document ownership checks remain in place for document management operations.
- Qdrant deletion filters by document ID, which is generated as a UUID.
- Existing vectors that lack the new owner and indexed metadata are excluded from tenant-filtered retrieval.

## 7. Known limitations

- Ingestion is synchronous, so large PDFs may hold an HTTP request open. A background worker would improve reliability and responsiveness at scale.
- The storage implementation is local disk; the storage interface exists, but no object-storage backend is implemented.
- Scanned PDFs without extractable text fail ingestion; OCR is not implemented.
- `retrieve_chunks` still permits calls without a `user_id` for legacy internal callers. The HTTP `/ask` route supplies one, but making the service parameter mandatory remains a useful hardening step.
- Legacy Qdrant points without owner/indexed metadata need reingestion before they can be used by the secured search path.
- Search currently covers all indexed documents belonging to the user. Conversation-specific document scopes are not implemented in this phase.

